    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Parks",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7761\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act authorizing the county of Saratoga to\ntransfer and convey reforested lands on Kinns Road to the town of\nClifton Park, county of Saratoga\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo ensure the continued use and preservation of Kinns Road Park in the\nTown of Clifton Park, Saratoga County.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of the bill authorizes Saratoga County to discontinue the\nuse of a parcel of reforested land on Kinns Road in the Town of\nClifton Park, and to convey its interest in such land to the Town of\nClifton Park for continued park and recreational purposes. It provides\nthat the Town shall continue to provide access to such land and\nrecreational facilities to all county residents. It further provides\nthat such lands shall be devoted for park and recreational purposes in\nperpetuity, and the conveyance of such lands shall be done without\ncompensation and shall not require any replacement of reforested\nlands.\n\nSection two identifies the specific parcel of land affected.\n\nSection three provides that the land to be transferred, and all\nstructures and facilities on such land, shall be maintained, owned and\noperated for park purposes by the Town.\n\nSection four provides that the use of such parkland and facilities\nshall be available to the general public.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis legislation was requested by both the County of Saratoga and the\nTown of Clifton Park. It would provide for the transfer of reforested\nlands currently owned by the County to the Town, for use as parklands.\n\nIn 1938, the County acquired a 631/2 acre parcel of land on Kinns Road\nin the Town of Clifton Park. Since 1940, this land has been managed in\naccordance with the reforested land provisions of Section 219 of the\nCounty Law.\n\nBeginning in the mid-1970s, there has been increased interest in the\nuse of such land for park and recreational purposes. Subsequently, the\nTown established non-motorized trails and a picnic area on these\nlands, and it became commonly known as Kinns Road Park. The park has\nbecome very popular in the community, and the trails are widely used\nby hikers, runners and others.\n\nA recent plan to harvest selected timber on the property generated\nmuch opposition and raised concerns about the use and preservation of\nthese lands, which technically remain \"reforested lands.\" As a result\nof these concerns, the County and Town have agreed on a plan to help\nprotect such lands and ensure their continued use for park and\nrecreational purposes.\n\nThis bill is based on previous laws which have authorized the transfer\nof parkland from one municipality to another.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone to the State\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately\n",
